# GPU Cluster Fault Correlation and Drain Scheduler

You are on call for a synthetic GPU training cluster. A distributed training run is showing NCCL timeouts, degraded bandwidth, and node health alerts. The current fault pipeline over-drains nodes and misses cross-layer root causes.

Your task is to repair the fault correlation and drain scheduler so the system can identify true root causes and produce safe operator-facing actions.

## What You Are Building

- telemetry ingestion
- cross-layer fault correlation
- root-cause classification
- safe drain scheduling
- on-call summary report

## What Is Provided

- synthetic GPU metrics inspired by DCGM-style telemetry
- synthetic NCCL all-reduce logs
- synthetic InfiniBand counter deltas
- synthetic thermal readings
- cluster topology and Slurm schedule fixtures
- local Docker simulator exposing the telemetry through HTTP
- public tests and expected output schema

## Main Files To Edit

- `src/telemetry_loader.py`
- `src/topology.py`
- `src/fault_engine.py`
- `src/drain_scheduler.py`

## Commands

```bash
make dev
make seed
make test
make test-integration
make run
make clean
```

`make dev` starts the local simulator. `make seed` verifies the simulator is ready and clears previous drain decisions. `make run` writes `results/fault_report.json` and `results/summary.md`.

## Deliverables

- fixed fault correlation and drain scheduling implementation
- `results/fault_report.json`
- `results/summary.md`

## Evaluation

Public tests check the visible contract. Private tests use harder telemetry to check GPU-vs-switch disambiguation, thermal cascades, missing telemetry confidence, Slurm-aware timing, and idempotent drain decisions.

No real GPUs, cloud credentials, Slurm cluster, NCCL install, or NVIDIA driver stack is required.
